# The site body, shared by every TLS strategy and imported into each
# Caddyfile.<strategy>. One copy: the strategies differ only in where the
# certificate comes from, and routing is not theirs to vary.
#
# Caddy is the only public HTTP service and it terminates TLS, nothing more. It
# does not validate tokens and does not synthesize identity headers, so no proxy
# header setting is an authorization boundary here.

# Only the documented routes are proxied. Anything else never reaches the broker
# at all, so this list has to move whenever the broker's router does: a route it
# omits is a 404 at the edge, in every deployment, that no test reaching the
# broker directly can see. The first segment is the source name (main.toml's
# `sources`, `[A-Za-z0-9._-]+`); `path` cannot express "one segment, then one of
# these names", so this is a regexp. `/{source}/devices/*` is
# `DELETE /{source}/devices/{id}`; a deeper path reaches the broker and is
# refused there.
# `/config` without a segment is the address an SRV record can express; the
# broker answers it only where one source makes the answer unambiguous.
@api path_regexp ^(/config|/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+/(config|ticket|nonce|devices)(/[^/]+)?)$
handle @api {
	# A ticket response carries a ccache; a request carries a bearer token.
	# Neither belongs in a log.
	request_body {
		max_size 16KB
	}
	reverse_proxy {$BROKER_UPSTREAM}
}

# One static page, for the person who types this address into a browser -- an
# address they saw in a dialog, or in a helper that is not working. A 404 there
# reads as "the service is broken"; caddy/site/index.html says what the address
# is for and what to do instead. Served by Caddy from a read-only mount, so it
# reaches neither the broker nor any identity: exactly `/` matches, and every
# other path still 404s.
@root path /
handle @root {
	root * /etc/caddy/site
	file_server
}

handle {
	respond 404
}
